{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_threshold",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "slope_change",
      "parent_relation": "The parent maps oxygen partial pressure to saturation through one global reduced-pressure scale P_O2/P50_O2 shared by all occupancy terms, so one refitted parameter set enforces a single characteristic half-saturation threshold and a tightly linked low-pressure rise and midrange steepness.",
      "child_relation": "Release the assumption that the half-saturation scale is fixed across all samples by allowing P50_O2 to become an independently varied condition; the saturation curve then shifts horizontally with P50_O2, changing the apparent low-to-mid pressure slope at a given P_O2 while preserving bounded monotone loading.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "P_O2",
        "P50_O2"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At the same imposed P_O2, samples with larger P50_O2 are less saturated, and the pressure range over which the curve rises is shifted to higher values; one parent parameter set with fixed P50_O2 cannot reproduce both left-shifted and right-shifted families simultaneously."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "reduced_pressure",
        "expression": "P_O2/P50_O2",
        "role": "dimensionless oxygen pressure relative to the sample-specific half-saturation scale"
      },
      {
        "name": "log_reduced_pressure",
        "expression": "log(P_O2/P50_O2)",
        "role": "coordinate on which horizontal shifts of the equilibrium binding curve become visually separable"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when equilibrated samples span materially different effective affinity scales, i.e. P50_O2 varies over a non-negligible range relative to sampled P_O2 so that P_O2/P50_O2 crosses order-unity values within the dataset.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, varying P_O2 already reveals the sigmoidal loading law; with additional variation in P50_O2, holdout data show systematic horizontal displacement of that same curve family rather than a single universal threshold.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent by holding P50_O2 constant across all samples.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_input",
    "promoted_symbol": "P50_O2",
    "promotion_reason": "A fixed P50_O2 hard-codes one affinity scale for the entire dataset, so the parent cannot represent admissible cross-sample horizontal shifts of the equilibrium saturation curve without separate refits; making P50_O2 an input is the minimal added freedom.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes one universal oxygen affinity scale for all sampled equilibrated blood conditions, excluding realistic sample-to-sample variation in the effective half-saturation pressure.",
    "baseline_assumption": "P50_O2 is constant across all observations rather than an independently imposed or measured sample condition.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Effective hemoglobin oxygen affinity can differ between equilibrated samples, producing horizontal shifts of the oxygen dissociation curve while retaining the same cooperative binding structure at fixed composition within each sample.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "P_O2",
      "P50_O2"
    ],
    "rationale": "The parent already captures cooperative equilibrium loading versus oxygen partial pressure at fixed conditions, but it uses a single global threshold parameter P50_O2. A realistic next refinement is to relax that fixed-environment assumption and let P50_O2 vary across samples as an input summarizing admissible affinity differences. The varied conditions are P_O2 and the promoted P50_O2; the within-sample temperature, acidity, carbon dioxide tension, and hemoglobin composition remain fixed when each curve is interpreted, and the target remains the instantaneous equilibrium saturation itself. The concrete contrast is that two sample groups with different P50_O2 values have the same bounded monotone sigmoidal shape in reduced-pressure coordinates but are horizontally shifted in physical-pressure coordinates; at a common P_O2 near the order-unity range of P_O2/P50_O2, the lower-affinity group is measurably less saturated. A single parent parameter set with fixed P50_O2 cannot reproduce both groups simultaneously because changing hill_coefficient, adair_alpha2, or adair_alpha3 alters curvature and occupancy weighting but does not create independent sample-specific threshold shifts. This refinement changes the scored mapping from supplied observations to S_O2, not merely an auxiliary quantity. The limitation is that if all samples truly share one affinity scale, the promoted input is inactive and the parent is recovered exactly."
  },
